   <h2><a name="INTRODUCTION"></a>Introduction</h2>

   This essay will get you started writing HTML so you can put up a website on the
   Internet.

   HTML stands for <b>H</b>yper<b>T</b>ext <b>M</b>arkup <b>L</b>anguage,a platform
   independent technique of distributing formatted documents via the web. The <b>bold</b>,
   <i>italic</i> etc. in the document you are reading now (presumably on a web
   browser), is encoded by embedding tags like &lt;B&gt; and &lt;I&gt;. This markup
   scheme works on any brand of computer and allows web sites to send all
   information in a standard way, without having to worry about what brand of
   computer the recipient has, or what software she uses.

   <h2><a name="LEARNING"></a>Learning HTML</h2>

   I found the easiest way to learn HTML is to look at other people's examples, to
   cut and paste from them, and to experiment by fiddling the various parameters to
   see what the visual effects are.

   <h2><a name="DOCUMENTATION"></a>Documentation</h2>

   It might help to buy an introductory text, but HTML is so simple, that probably
   is not necessary.

   <ul>

      <li>
         The <a href="http://werbach.com/barebones/">bare bones html guide</a>.
      </li>

      <li>
         the classic <a href="http://www.ncsa.uiuc.edu/General/Internet/WWW/HTMLPrimerAll.html">A
         Beginner's Guide to HTML</a>
      </li>

      <li>
         The <a href="http://www.ncsa.uiuc.edu/SDG/Software/Mosaic/Forms/">tutorial for
         FORMS</a>.
      </li>

      <li>
         For the masochists, try the official standards documents for <a href="http://www.w3.org/pub/WWW/TR/REC-html32.html">HTML
         3.2</a> and <a href="http://www.w3.org/TR/html401/">HTML 4.01</a>.
      </li>

   </ul>

   <h2><a name="PROOFREADING"></a>Proofreading</h2>

   To ensure your HTML will work when you upload it to a website server, use all
   lowercase filenames and directory names. Avoid spaces and punctuation (except _)
   in names. Make sure you use relative links to your own files -- no C: or file:///C|/
   style absolute references.

   <p>

   The W3 Consortium offers an <a href="http://validator.w3.org/">
   <img src="images/w3clogo91x48.gif" border="0" height="48" width="91" alt="W3C_logo" align="middle">
   online validator</a> for the various HTML dialects. It is sort of a Lint for
   HTML. It can ensure your HTML will work properly on browsers other than the one
   you tested it on. The W3C consortium also controls the various HTML standards.

   <p>

   For speed, and control, I use <a href="http://www.htmlvalidator.com/">
   <img src="images/htmlvalidated.gif" alt="HTML Checked!" border="0" height="31" width="88" align="middle">
   &nbsp;CSE HTMLValidator</a> to check my web pages offline and in batches.

   <p>

   When it comes to HTML4, and <a href="jglosscr.html#CSS">CSS style sheets</a>,
   browser support is shaky. You have to stick to a <a href="http://webreview.com/wr/pub/guides/style/safegrid.html">subset
   of features</a> that are commonly supported.

   <h1>The Tags</h1>

   This section just summarised the tags.  Sometimes what I tell you here will be
   enough to use them. It is really just designed to jog your memory.  Look
   elsewhere for details or <b>experiment</b>!

   <h2><a name="CLASS"></a>Class</h2>

   It is easiest to use the class attribute, then specify what it means in your <a href="jglosscr.html#CSS">CSS
   style sheet</a>.

   <table border="1" cellspacing="1" bordercolor="#808080" cellpadding="7">
      <colgroup span="2" class="html">
      </colgroup>

      <thead>

      <tr>
         <th colspan="3">Use of CSS class</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <th>Start Tag</th>

         <th>End Tag</th>

         <th>description</th>
      </tr>

      </thead>

      <tbody>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;span class=&quot;strawberry&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/span&gt;</td>

         <td>encloses text of the strawberry class.  It will look in the style sheet to
         figure out what attriutes should be applied to strawberry text, perhaps a size,
         colour, alignment, font etc.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;ul class=&quot;strawberry&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/ul&gt;</td>

         <td>like a regular UL except everything in it is shoud be treated as strawberry
         text.</td>
      </tr>

      </tbody>

   </table>

   <h2><a name="GROUPS"></a>Groups</h2>

   <table border="1" cellspacing="1" bordercolor="#808080" cellpadding="7">
      <colgroup span="2" class="html">
      </colgroup>
      <thead>

      <tr>
         <th colspan="3">Groups, Lists, Glossaries</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <th>Start Tag</th>

         <th>End Tag</th>

         <th>description</th>
      </tr>

      </thead>
      <tbody>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;ol&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/ol&gt;</td>

         <td>ordered numbered list</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;ul&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/ul&gt;</td>

         <td>unordered bulleted list</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;menu&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/menu&gt;</td>

         <td>menu list, more compact than ul.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;li&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/li&gt;</td>

         <td>list item</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;dl&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/dl&gt;</td>

         <td>dictionary list</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;dt&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/dt&gt;</td>

         <td>dictionary term being defined</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;dd&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/dd&gt;</td>

         <td>dictionary definition</td>
      </tr>

      </tbody>

   </table>

   <h2><a name="LINEBREAKS"></a>Line Breaks</h2>

   <table border="1" cellspacing="1" bordercolor="#808080" cellpadding="7">
      <colgroup span="1" class="html">
      </colgroup>
      <thead>

      <tr>
         <th colspan="2">Line Breaks</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <th>Start Tag</th>

         <th>description</th>
      </tr>

      </thead>
      <tbody>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;br&gt;</td>

         <td>new line, no extra space</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;br clear=&quot;all&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>gets past any flow-around illustration.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;p&gt;</td>

         <td>new paragraph, blank line inserted</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;p align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;hr&gt;</td>

         <td>horizontal rule</td>
      </tr>

      </tbody>

   </table>

   <h2><a name="FONTSELECTORS"></a>Font selectors</h2>

   <table border="1" cellspacing="1" bordercolor="#808080" cellpadding="7">
      <colgroup span="2" class="html">
      </colgroup>
      <thead>

      <tr>
         <th colspan="3">Font Colours and Size</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <th>Start Tag</th>

         <th>End Tag</th>

         <th>description</th>
      </tr>

      </thead>
      <tbody>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;h1&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/h1&gt;</td>

         <td>major heading</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;h6&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/h6&gt;</td>

         <td>most minor heading</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;b&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/b&gt;</td>

         <td>bold</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;i&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/i&gt;</td>

         <td>italic</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;tt&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/tt&gt;</td>

         <td>typewriter font</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;pre&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/pre&gt;</td>

         <td>preformatted</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;font size=&quot;+3&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/font&gt;</td>

         <td>or 3 for absolute size rather than increase</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;font color=&quot;red&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/font&gt;</td>

         <td>see choice of <a href="htmlcheat.html#COLOURS">colours</a>.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;font face=&quot;Comic Sans MS,Helvetica&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/font&gt;</td>

         <td>suggest a typeface.  User must have it installed, can specify alternates in
         order of preference.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;big&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/big&gt;</td>

         <td>shorthand for &lt;font size=&quot;+1&quot;&gt;</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;small&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/small&gt;</td>

         <td>shorthand for &lt;font size=&quot;-1&quot;&gt;</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;dfn&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/dfn&gt;</td>

         <td>definition</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;em&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/em&gt;</td>

         <td>italic emphasis</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;cite&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/cite&gt;</td>

         <td>book titles</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;code&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/code&gt;</td>

         <td>program listings</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;kbd&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/kbd&gt;</td>

         <td>keystrokes</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;samp&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/samp&gt;</td>

         <td>computer status messages</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;strong&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/strong&gt;</td>

         <td>bold emphasis</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;var&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/var&gt;</td>

         <td>to be replaced by specific when used. Typically rendered in italics.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;u&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/u&gt;</td>

         <td>underline</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;address&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/address&gt;</td>

         <td>email address, possibly street address.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;blockquote&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/blockquote&gt;</td>

         <td>long quotation</td>
      </tr>

      </tbody>

   </table>

   <h2><a name="ANCHORS"></a>Anchors</h2>

   typical target -- place where you jump TO:

   <ul class="html">
      &lt;H2&gt;&lt;a NAME=&quot;GLOSSARY&quot;&gt;Roedy's Java Glossary&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;

   </ul>

   <p>

   <h2><a name="LINKS"></a>Links</h2>

   typical reference that when clicked takes you to a particular spot:

   <ul class="html">
      &lt;a href=&quot;http://www.Delftware.com/roedy/gloss.htm#GLOSSARY&quot;&gt;glossary&lt;/a&gt;

      <br>

      or

      <br>

      &lt;a href=&quot;gloss.htm#GLOSSARY&quot;&gt;glossary&lt;/a&gt;

      <br>

      or

      <br>

      &lt;a href=&quot;#GLOSSARY&quot;&gt;glossary&lt;/a&gt;

   </ul>

   Email reference

   <ul class="html">
      &lt;a href=&quot;mailto:roedy@mindprod.com&quot;&gt;Roedy Green : roedy@mindprod.com&lt;/a&gt;

   </ul>

   It is a good idea to repeat the email address visibly for people whose browsers
   don't support mailto or for people who want to copy/paste the email address into
   their separate email or database program.

   <p>

   GIF or JPG image reference (often embedded in an
   <span class="html">&lt;a href=&quot;someplace.html&quot;&gt; .. &lt;/a&gt;</span>)
   clickable link to provide a picture button:

   <ul class="html">
      &lt;img src=&quot;email.gif&quot; width=&quot;44&quot; height=&quot;33&quot;
      align=&quot;middle&quot; alt=&quot;Email logo&quot;

      longdesc=&quot;http://www.thismachine.com/cashflowchar.txt&quot;&gt;

   </ul>

   The longdesc points to a text description of the image for the visually disabled.

   <h2><a name="TABLES"></a>Tables</h2>

   The trick to understanding tables is to realise that the entire table must be
   contained in
   <span class="html">&lt;table&gt;...&lt;/table&gt;</span>, each row must be
   contained in
   <span class="html">&lt;tr&gt; ... &lt;/tr&gt;</span>, each heading must be
   contained in
   <span class="html">&lt;th&gt; ... &lt;/th&gt;</span> and each data cell must be
   contained in
   <span class="html">&lt;td&gt; ... &lt;/td&gt;</span>.

   <table border="1" cellspacing="1" bordercolor="#808080" cellpadding="7">
      <colgroup span="2" class="html">
      </colgroup>
      <thead>

      <tr>
         <th colspan="3">Tables</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <th>Start Tag</th>

         <th>End Tag</th>

         <th>description</th>
      </tr>

      </thead>
      <tbody>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;table border=&quot;5&quot;

            <br>

            cellspacing=&quot;2&quot;

            <br>

            cellpadding=&quot;1&quot;

            <br>

            width=&quot;100&quot;

            <br>

            width=&quot;50%&quot;

            <br>

            bgcolor=&quot;#f0e68c&quot;

            <br>

            summary=&quot;Best places to buy toasters&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/table&gt;</td>

         <td>cellpadding is interior border of cell.

            <br>

            cellspacing is border _between_ cells, the width of the horizontal and vertical
            lines separating cells.

            <br>

            cellpadding will appear on left side of leftmost cell and right side of the
            rightmost cell; cellspacing will not. Summary does not display.  It is used
            primarily to help visually disabled people navigate.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;caption&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/caption&gt;</td>

         <td>caption title, appears above table, in plain font.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;colgroup span=&quot;2&quot; class=&quot;strawberry&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/colgroup&gt;</td>

         <td>encloses &lt;col&gt; elements.  Also can be used to apply attributes to a
         group of columns. Not supported in Opera 5. Must come before everything else.

            HTML 4 lets you specify the attributes of an entire column. You don't have to
            repeat them in every cell the way you do in regular HTML. See the <a href="http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/struct/tables.html#h-11.2.4">COL
            and COLGROUP elements</a>. Have a look at this <a href="http://www.subotnik.net/tmp/raggettables.html">example</a>.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;col class=&quot;strawberry&quot;  align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>tells about entire column's colour, alignment, class, colspan etc. Note you
         put everying right in the &lt;col&gt; tag.  There is no &lt;/col&gt; tag.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;thead&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/thead&gt;</td>

         <td>encloses table header rows. The THEAD TBODY and TFOOT tags let you group
         your table in three regions, so that if the table scrolls, the header and footer
         will always be visible.  Just the middle part will scroll.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;tbody&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/tbody&gt;</td>

         <td>encloses table body rows</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;tr valign=&quot;baseline&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/tr&gt;</td>

         <td>table row valign can be top, middle, bottom, baseline. valign can also occur
         on caption, tr and td.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;th align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/th&gt;</td>

         <td>header item, also left,center,right,justify</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>data item</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;th rowspan=&quot;2&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/th&gt;</td>

         <td>header spanning two rows</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td rowspan=&quot;2&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>data item spanning two rows</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;th colspan=&quot;2&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/th&gt;</td>

         <td>header spanning two columns</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td colspan=&quot;2&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>data item spanning two columns</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td dp=&quot;:&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>align data in this cell at the : char</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td nowrap&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>may not break lines in cell</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;td width=&quot;50%&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/td&gt;</td>

         <td>hints on how to compute column widths</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;tfoot&gt;</td>

         <td>&lt;/tfoot&gt;</td>

         <td>encloses table footer rows</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;spacer type=horizonal&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>can also be vertical or block. Netscape appears to ignore spacers.</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;spacer size=&quot;20&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>size in pixels</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;spacer width=&quot;20&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>horizontal size in pixels</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;spacer height=&quot;20&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>vertical size in pixels</td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
         <td>&lt;spacer align=&quot;left&quot;&gt;</td>

         <td>&nbsp;</td>

         <td>also right and center</td>
      </tr>

      </tbody>

   </table>

